WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.120
Rampant cannibalism, a horrific capture, and&nbsp;
a folk tale that's lived on for centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.500
"The Hills Have Eyes" was inspired by&nbsp;
the tale of a truly terrifying family.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.900
1977's "The Hills Have Eyes" was only&nbsp;
Wes Craven's second movie, and it helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.700
establish him as a well-respected name in horror&nbsp;
circles. It already bore many of the hallmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.560
that would make Craven a master of the genre,&nbsp;
such as frank depictions of brutal violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.258
a distinct dash of social satire, and&nbsp;
a wry view of America's darker sides.

00:00:31.258 --> 00:00:33.929
"Maybe there's some rattlesnakes prowling around."

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:37.980
Craven was also known to draw inspiration&nbsp;
for his movies from real life. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.660
"A Nightmare on Elm Street" was inspired by a Los&nbsp;
Angeles Times story about a young boy who was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.820
son of refugees from the Cambodian killing fields.&nbsp;
As Craven told Vulture, this boy's nightmares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.340
apparently became so extreme that he died in&nbsp;
his sleep, and thus, Freddy Krueger was born.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.980
For "The Hills Have Eyes," Craven drew inspiration&nbsp;
from a true story of a suburban family set upon by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.380
rural cannibals. As recounted by Thommy Hutson in&nbsp;
the documentary "Never Sleep Again: The Elm Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.580
Legacy," Craven found the inspiration for "The&nbsp;
Hills Have Eyes" when he encountered the Scottish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.180
legend of Sawney Bean while conducting&nbsp;
research at the New York Public Library.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.580
As the legend goes, Alexander "Sawney"&nbsp;
Bean was born in the East Lothian region of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.080
Scotland sometime in the 16th century.&nbsp;
A disturbed and violent young man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.520
he was lucky enough to find a wife who&nbsp;
shared his disturbing predilections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.980
a woman named "Black Agnes" Douglas. Unwilling&nbsp;
to conform to the world around them or deny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:37.260
their base cravings for human flesh, the two&nbsp;
retreated to a cave in the countryside. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.360
they had several children and, through&nbsp;
incestuous relationships, a few dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.760
grandchildren. In total, the cave-dwelling&nbsp;
clan was said to have been almost fifty-strong.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.880
The story goes that the Bean clan survived by&nbsp;
ambushing, killing, and then eating travelers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.700
and various passersby. Most often, victims were&nbsp;
dismembered in the cave. After they were done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.380
eating, the Beans would allegedly pickle what&nbsp;
was left over in barrels. Over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:10.680
approximately 25 years, the Bean family were said&nbsp;
to have killed and eaten over a thousand people.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.140
The Beans were only stopped when King James VI&nbsp;
of Scotland was alerted to the dangerous clan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.480
The king allegedly assembled a team of 400 men to&nbsp;
hunt down the Sawney Bean family — and according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.400
to the BBC, every member of the family was&nbsp;
executed. Though the story features prominently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.440
in Scottish legend, there is no real proof that&nbsp;
the Sawney Bean clan ever actually existed.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.600
What seems to have fascinated Wes Craven about&nbsp;
the story wasn't just the sheer brutality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.920
Bean family's actions, but the brutality&nbsp;
of their executions. According to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.240
versions of the story, the men of the Bean&nbsp;
family were mutilated and left to bleed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.660
death in front of the women and children,&nbsp;
who were then burned alive themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.580
per The Scotsman. In a 1977 interview&nbsp;
with Arrow magazine, Craven explained,

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.640
This conflict gave Craven the idea for one of the&nbsp;
signatures of "The Hills Have Eyes." In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.980
the surviving members of the supposedly civilized&nbsp;
Carter and Wood families end up resorting to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.420
same viciousness as the hill folk who have&nbsp;
attacked them. By cleverly exposing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.680
brutal behavior, Craven reveals that there's&nbsp;
very little difference between the families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.020
and that, deep down, all humans are capable&nbsp;
of committing shockingly dark deeds.

